<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleppo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aleppo is a major city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> with a long history as a trading and political centre. It was a key prize in the twelfth-century struggles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, whose control of it was central to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>. Ayyubid princes ruled it until the Mongol sack of 1260.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleppo.jpg" alt="Aleppo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleppo_Citadel_entrance_-_seen_from_southeast_9933.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The confederation also controlled important stretches of the caravan routes that carried Iranian raw silk westwards to Bursa and <b>Aleppo</b>, and Tabriz, its capital after 1469, was one of the great commercial cities of western Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Smbat's embassy to Karakorum brought back in 1250 a guarantee of Cilicia's integrity; Het'um I visited Möngke Khan in 1253 and fought under Hulagu at <b>Aleppo</b> and Damascus in 1259–1260, where Arab historians charge his troops with a massacre and arson in the main mosque.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…took Damascus that autumn, progressively absorbed the Zengid principalities of Syria and northern Mesopotamia through a combination of military pressure and diplomacy, sent his brother Turanshah to conquer Yemen in 1174, securing the Red Sea route, took <b>Aleppo</b> in 1183 and received the submission of Mosul in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Visitors found the Hetmanate unusually literate: in the 1740s 866 of 1,099 settlements in seven regimental districts had primary schools, more per head than in Russia or Poland, and Paul of <b>Aleppo</b> wrote in 1654 that even wives and daughters could read.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ebla lay at Tell Mardikh, about 55 kilometres south-west of <b>Aleppo</b> in northern Syria, on the rolling plain between the Orontes valley and the Euphrates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ebla">Ebla · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His dismantling of the Mitanni kingdom and installation of Hittite-aligned rulers across Syria, including his own sons at Carchemish and <b>Aleppo</b>, established the imperial framework that defined the empire at its height.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
